  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Noturus miurus Jordan, 1877. Size: 130 mm (5.1\") SL. Type locality: White River, near Indianapolis, Marion County, Indiana, U.S.A.</p><p>Identification: light yellow or brown above with dark mottling; 4 vague saddles; white to yellow below; brown or black border on the caudal fin; brown or black mottling on the other fins. Head rounded above.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>North America: lower Great Lakes drainages in Ontario, Canada and New York, USA, southwest through most of the Ohio River basin and lower Mississippi River basin (west to eastern Kansas and Oklahoma); Mohawk River in New York; Pearl River and Lake Pontchartrain drainages in Mississippi and Louisiana, USA.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Inhabits riffles, pools below riffles and runs over gravel and sand mixed with sticks and leaves in creeks and small rivers. Also found in lakes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<ul><li>pH: 6.2 - 7.0</li><li>Temperature: 14.0-22.0°C (57.2-71.6°F)</li><li>Well oxygenated water should be maintained.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Provide sand mixed with peat moss, PVC pipes, flat rocks and vegetation.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Readily feeds on live and frozen foods. Will also adapt to flake and prepared foods for the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Suitable for a coldwater community tank. Ideal tank mates include small to midsized North American native fishes such as darters and shiners.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Breeding male with swollen lips, enlarged cephalic epaxial muscles, and large bulbous genital papilla. Female with papilla with deep median groove ending in a small bump.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Spawning occurs in the months of May-August. It is difficult in the aquarium, however it has been done when wild caught specimens are added to aquariums with warmer temperatures than the river or drainage system they are captured from.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: from the Greek notos, meaning back, and oura, meaning tail, in reference to the confluent adipose and caudal fins. The specific epithet refers to the stout appearance of some specimens.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1045",
      "html": "<p>Annals of the Lyceum of Natural History of New York v. 11 (nos 11-12) (art. 29), pp 371.</p>"
    }
  ],
